{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189754": {
      "title": "Visiting Professor of Political Science",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Social Sciences: Government Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Government at Dartmouth College invites applications for a Visiting Professor from Israel to conduct research and teach in the department during the 2027–28 academic year. Candidates who are pursuing research and teaching related to Israel’s domestic politics or foreign/security relations, broadly construed, are particularly encouraged to apply. The appointment is for 1 or 2 terms during any of Dartmouth’s four 10-week quarters.  The successful candidate will teach 1 undergraduate course, related to Israeli politics, for each academic term in residence. Ideally applicants will have experience teaching a version of the proposed course. The visiting professor will give a public lecture while at Dartmouth. The candidate’s research and teaching interests would ideally position them to develop productive relationships with faculty and postdoctoral fellows in Dartmouth’s intellectual community. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Ph.D. in Political Science or a related field. Applicants must have a research/teaching position at an Israeli university. Former practitioners without a doctorate but with relevant research and teaching experience may be considered. This position is not eligible for H-1B sponsorship. Please submit all materials electronically via Interfolio: Cover letter. Curriculum Vitae, Evidence of teaching effectiveness (eg: evaluations and/or syllabi), Statement of research plans, One (1) writing sample. The cover letter should indicate whether the candidate proposes to spend 1 or 2 terms in residence at Dartmouth, or whether they are open to both options Review of applications will begin September 4, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be addressed to Professor Jeffrey Friedman, at Jeffrey.A.Friedman@dartmouth.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194447": {
      "title": "Economics Research Specialist in International Trade",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Economics at Dartmouth College invites applications for an Economics Research Specialist (ERS), at the rank of Post Graduate Research Associate. The ERS will join the Michael G. Fisch 1983 Academic Cluster on Globalization in the area of international economics.  The ERS will work under the supervision of the faculty members affiliated with the Cluster, including Treb Allen (Economics), Davin Chor (Tuck School of Business), and Yang-Yang Zhou (Government). Responsibilities include: Assisting Globalization Cluster faculty with research, including processing and analyzing large datasets and coding necessary to implement related economic and econometric models. Participating in weekly internal and external seminars of the Globalization Cluster. Administrative support for Globalization Cluster, including updating the cluster website and helping to coordinate the seminars. Economics Research Specialists work with department faculty supporting and conducting scholarly research and teaching in anticipation of advancing their skills and experience to prepare for the next stage of their careers. ERSs are expected to contribute to all stages of research: research design, literature searches, conducting experiments, collecting and analyzing data, and writing reports and papers. ERSs may also have opportunity to contribute in the classroom or in laboratory sections of courses, helping undergraduate students with labs, review sessions, paper editing, and grading certain assignments. This position is full-time, in-residence at Dartmouth College in Hanover, NH, with start date as early as January 2027. Appointments are typically for a 1-year period with the option of a 1-year extension. The starting annual salary is $60,000. ERSs who are working in residence in Hanover, NH are eligible for Graduate Special Student status and the ability to take certain Dartmouth courses with the tuition paid by Dartmouth as a working condition fringe benefit. The benefit covers a maximum of 1 course per academic year the ERSs is employed and in residence at Dartmouth. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their application letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Bachelor’s in economics or a related field, completed by the start of the appointment, and demonstrated excellence in academic performance Strong mathematical background (including multivariate calculus and linear algebra, with knowledge of higher-level math such as graph theory and differential equations) Strong programming skills (including Stata, Matlab) Familiarity with AI tools (including Claude); The ability to work independently; excellent analytical and critical thinking skills, and strong written and oral communication skills Preferred Qualifications Research Assistant experience Long-term interest in pursuing a Ph.D. in economics 1.  Application letter that addresses: A brief description of your experience as a research assistant and with any independent research (e.g. thesis or other research projects) A brief description of your mathematical background A description of your familiarity with various statistical, computer and AI packages (e.g. STATA, Matlab, Claude, etc.) Any other experiences relevant to the position description Date you are available to start work 2.  CV/Resume – please include: Name Email and phone/text contact Mailing Address Current position or academic affiliation Date of undergraduate degree (or expected date) Academic Concentration(s) 3.  Transcript (Unofficial is fine) 4. Contact information for professional references.  If applicable, please indicate which reference you have worked with as a research assistant Review of applications will begin October 16, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Recommendation letters will be requested only for finalists. For questions about the position, please contact Professor Davin Chor at: davin.chor@dartmouth.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194465": {
      "title": "Economics Research Specialist in Globalization",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Economics at Dartmouth College invites applications for an Economics Research Specialist (ERS), at the rank of Post Graduate Research Associate. The ERS will join the Michael G. Fisch 1983 Academic Cluster on Globalization in the areas of globalization and political economy.  The ERS will work under the supervision of the faculty members affiliated with the Cluster, including Treb Allen (Economics), Davin Chor (Tuck School of Business), and Yang-Yang Zhou (Government). Responsibilities include: Assisting Globalization Cluster faculty with research, including processing and analyzing large datasets and coding necessary to implement related economic and econometric models. Participating in weekly internal and external seminars of the Globalization Cluster. Administrative support for Globalization Cluster, including updating the cluster website and helping to coordinate the seminars. Economics Research Specialists work with department faculty supporting and conducting scholarly research and teaching in anticipation of advancing their skills and experience to prepare for the next stage of their careers. ERSs are expected to contribute to all stages of research, including research design, literature searches, conducting experiments, collecting and analyzing data, and writing reports and papers. ERSs may also have opportunity to contribute in the classroom or in laboratory sections of courses, helping undergraduate students with labs, review sessions, paper editing, and grading certain assignments. This position is full-time, in-residence at Dartmouth College in Hanover, NH, with start date as early as January 2027. Appointments are typically for a 1-year period with the option of a 1-year extension. The starting annual salary is $60,000. ERSs who are working in residence in Hanover, NH are eligible for Graduate Special Student status and the ability to take certain Dartmouth courses with the tuition paid by Dartmouth as a working condition fringe benefit. The benefit covers a maximum of 1 course per academic year the ERSs is employed and in residence at Dartmouth. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their application letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Bachelor’s degree in economics or a related field, completed by the start of the appointment, and demonstrated excellence in academic performance. Strong statistical/data science background and knowledge of causal inference techniques Strong programming skills in R and/or Python Familiarity working with AI tools (including Claude code) Interest in the social sciences (including topics related to migration, development, aid, and/or conflict) The ability to work independently; excellent analytical and critical thinking skills, and strong written and oral communication skills Preferred Qualifications Research Assistant experience Long-term interest in pursuing a Ph.D. in political science, economics, or a related field This position is eligible for J-1 visa sponsorship for eligible candidates. Employment is contingent on authorization to work in the United States. 1.  Application letter that addresses: A brief description of your experience as a research assistant and with any independent research (e.g. thesis or other research projects) A brief description of your mathematical background. A description of your familiarity with various statistical, computer and AI packages (e.g. R, Python, STATA, Claude Code, etc.) Any other experiences relevant to the position description Date available to start work 2.  CV/Resume - Please include: Name Email and phone/text contact Mailing Address Current position or academic affiliation Date of undergraduate degree (or expected date) Academic Concentration(s) 3.  Transcript (Unofficial is acceptable) 4.  Contact information for 3 professional references.  If applicable, please indicate which reference you have worked with as a research assistant Review of applications will begin October 16, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Recommendation letters will be requested only for finalists. For questions about the position, please contact Professor Yang-Yang Zhou: yang-yang.zhou@dartmouth.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194525": {
      "title": "Economics Research Specialist in Economics and Economic Policy",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Economics at Dartmouth College invites applications for an Economics Research Specialist, at the rank of Post Graduate Research Associate. The ERS will support Professor Heidi Williams with all stages of qualitative and quantitative research related to economics and economic policy. Responsibilities Programming to construct and validate data Undertaking descriptive and econometric analyses Estimate economic models Communication of qualitative and quantitative research in writing Assistance drafting and editing papers for publication Economics Research Specialists (ERSs) will work with department faculty supporting and conducting scholarly research and teaching in anticipation of advancing their skills and experience to prepare for the next stage of their careers. ERSs are expected to contribute to all stages of research – including research design, literature searches, conducting experiments, collecting and analyzing data and writing reports and papers. ERSs may also have opportunity to contribute in the classroom or in laboratory sections of courses, helping undergraduate students with labs, review sessions, paper editing and grading certain assignments. This position is full-time, expected to be non-remote, in-residence for the Department of Economics at Dartmouth College in Hanover, NH, with start date as early as June 2027. Remote positions may be possible. Appointments are typically for a 1-year period with the potential for renewal. Salary is $60,000 annually. ERSs who are working in residence in Hanover, NH are eligible for Graduate Special Student status and the ability to take certain Dartmouth courses with the tuition paid by Dartmouth as a working condition fringe benefit. The benefit covers a maximum of 1 course per academic year the ERSs is employed and in residence at Dartmouth. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their application letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Bachelor’s in economics or a related field, completed by the start of the appointment A long-term interest in pursuing research related to economics and economic policy Experience and interest in constructing and analyzing data but also in undertaking qualitative, institutional, and historical work needed to interpret the data in context. Previous programming experience (in languages such as R, Matlab, Python, or Stata) Demonstrated ability to work well collaboratively and independently Excellent attention to detail, problem solving skills, and creativity Strong writing and oral communication skills This position is eligible for J-1 visa sponsorship for eligible candidates. Employment is contingent on authorization to work in the United States. 1.  Application letter that addresses: Relevant research-related experience Teaching or mentoring experience Preparation to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all Educational and career goals and how the position would advance them. 2. CV/Resume Name Email and phone/text contact Current position, or academic affiliation Date of (expected) undergraduate degree Academic concentration(s) List all academic classes (and final grades) you have completed that focus primarily on economics or economic policy Include programming languages and/or software and levels of proficiency. 3.  Most recent academic transcript (unofficial transcript is acceptable) 4.  Three professional references with contact information Review of applications will be on a rolling basis and will continue until the position is filled. Recommendation letters will be requested only for finalists. For questions about the position, please contact Amy Rossi at amy.j.rossi@dartmouth.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192063": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Sciences: Chemistry Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Chemistry at Dartmouth invites applications for a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor, with a focus on synthetic organic chemistry. We seek applicants who will establish nationally recognized research programs with innovation in organic chemistry, with potential applicability towards, but not limited to, organic synthesis, reaction methodology, and catalysis. Qualified candidates should be prepared to teach graduate and undergraduate courses in organic chemistry. This position includes opportunities to collaborate on research projects within the department and with other Dartmouth researchers, including those at the Geisel School of Medicine, Dartmouth Hitchcock Medical Center, Dartmouth Cancer Center, Thayer School of Engineering, or the Irving Institute for Energy and Society. Graduate students and postdoctoral scholars are supported by the Guarini School for Graduate and Advanced Studies , including their community initiatives . We are especially interested in applicants who will contribute to Dartmouth research mentoring initiatives such as Early Research Access in the Sciences , the E.E. Just STEM Scholars Program and other Scholar Programs in STEM . Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Applicants must have completed a Ph.D. in Organic Chemistry or a closely related discipline. Responsibilities include research and classroom teaching for undergraduate and graduate students. Please submit all materials electronically via Interfolio. Letters may be addressed to Professor Ellen Sletten. Cover Letter, addressing all relevant aspects of the position description CV, including contact information for three references Research Statement, addressing accomplishments and future plans Teaching and Mentoring Statement, addressing past experiences and future plans Three letters of recommendation Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this position, please contact: Professor Ellen Sletten (ellen.m.sletten@dartmouth.edu), or department administrator Rebecca Talmadge (rebecca.j.talmadge@dartmouth.edu). This position is full-time, in residence, non-remote in Hanover, NH. For all openings in Arts and Sciences , please visit our Faculty Recruitment page."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192064": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Latin American Medical Humanities",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Arts and Humanities: Spanish and Portuguese Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Spanish and Portuguese at Dartmouth invites applications for a full-time tenure-track appointment as Assistant Professor in Latin American Medical Humanities. The position is open to specializations in any historical period and in any Spanish-speaking region of Latin America and/or the Caribbean, including hemispheric, transatlantic, and transpacific comparative approaches. Applicants for this position should demonstrate how their scholarship and teaching engage with the intersections of language, literature, culture, and history with health, wellness, and the body as they are defined by scientific and medical discourses. A strong theoretical foundation and full professional fluency in Spanish are required. In addition to teaching courses in their area of expertise, all faculty members are expected to teach all levels of literature, culture, and language and participate in the department’s extensive study abroad programs. Our faculty regularly collaborate with other departments and programs, such as the Latin American, Latino, and Caribbean Studies Department, the African and African American Studies Department, the Comparative Literature Program, and the Women’s, Gender, and Sexualities Studies Program. We also collaborate with colleagues through programming and initiatives at the Leslie Center for the Humanities and the Dartmouth Center for Social Impact. Faculty in Spanish and Portuguese are engaged in mentoring undergraduate and graduate students as well as postdoctoral fellows through the Mellon Mays Undergraduate Fellowship Program, the Comparative Literature MA Program, and the Provost’s Fellowship Program. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. PhD, or ABD with PhD received by the time of appointment, in Latin American Literary and Cultural Studies, or a closely related field within the Arts and Humanities. Responsibilities include research and classroom instruction of undergraduate students. A strong record of scholarly research with evident promise for sustained, high-impact academic contributions. Full professional fluency in Spanish, with demonstrated experience teaching language and/or literature courses at the university level. Ability to teach a broad range of courses, including language instruction and survey courses in Spanish/Latin American literatures and cultural studies. Capacity to implement and direct Dartmouth’s off-campus programs in Latin America and Spain. Proven excellence in undergraduate teaching and mentorship, coupled with evident preparation to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Applicants are encouraged to spell out plans for building a vigorous research program, to elucidate ideas for effective classroom teaching and mentoring, and to discuss goals for contributing to the growth and support of a diverse community of students and scholars on our campus. Please submit all materials electronically via Interfolio. Letters may be addressed to Professor Israel Reyes. Cover letter (maximum two, single-spaced pages) CV, including contact information for three references Research statement, including past accomplishments and future plans (maximum two, single-spaced pages) Teaching statement (maximum one, single-spaced page) Mentoring statement, including preparation to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. (maximum one, single-spaced page) Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Additional materials, such as letters of recommendation and a writing sample, may be requested at a later date. For questions regarding this position, please contact: Department Administrator Greg LeBlanc at Greg.LeBlanc@dartmouth.edu . This position is full-time, in residence, non-remote in Hanover, NH. For all openings in Arts and Sciences , please visit our Faculty Recruitment page."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192850": {
      "title": "Chilcott Professor in Biochemistry and Cell Biology, Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth, Tenure Track",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Department of Biochemistry & Cell Biology Location: Hanover, New Hampshire The Department of Biochemistry & Cell Biology at Dartmouth’s Geisel School of Medicine invites applications for the James C. Chilcott 1920 Professorship. We seek internationally recognized candidates at the Full Professor level with outstanding records of solving broad biological problems. Exceptional candidates at the Associate Professor rank will also be considered. The department is a vibrant and highly collegial scientific community comprised of multiple NAS members and AAAS fellows with IP-endowed resources. This position aligns with recently announced institutional research priorities for transformational investment. A highly competitive recruitment package will be provided, including sustained resources to support an ambitious research program and access to state-of-the-art research facilities. Dartmouth is a world-class research institution that ranks among top R1 schools in per capita research spending. Department members benefit from extensive multidisciplinary resources including the Molecular and Cellular Biology Graduate Program , the Institute for Biomolecular Targeting COBRE, the Dartmouth Cancer Center , and more. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. People with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives make the Dartmouth community diverse, which enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to advance Dartmouth's commitment to diversity in service of academic excellence. The Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth is located in the neighboring towns of Hanover and Lebanon in the picturesque Upper Connecticut River Valley on the NH and VT border. Dartmouth is surrounded by a vibrant, academic and professional community offering excellent schools, lively arts, and an unmatched quality of life in a beautiful, rural setting. Amenities associated with urban areas in Boston MA, Burlington VT, and Montreal, QC are all within a few hours’ drive. Dartmouth is dedicated to establishing and maintaining a safe and nondiscriminatory learning, living, and working environment in which all individuals are treated with respect and dignity. The College's Principle of Community establishes that interactions between and among members of the Dartmouth community should be based on integrity, responsibility, and consideration so that all may fully access and benefit from the opportunities the College provides. Dartmouth is committed to the principle of equal opportunity for all its students, faculty, staff, and applicants for admission and employment. For that reason, Dartmouth prohibits any form of discrimination against any person on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, gender identity or expression, pregnancy, age, sexual orientation, marital or parental status, national origin, citizenship, disability, genetic information, military or veteran status, or any other legally protected status in the administration of and access to the College's programs and activities, and in conditions of admission and employment. We are recruiting internationally recognized candidates at the Full Professor level with outstanding records of solving broad biological problems. Exceptional candidates at the Associate Professor rank will also be considered. Successful candidates are expected to demonstrate a history of high-impact discoveries, sustained external funding, and scholarly distinction. Applicants should have a PhD, MD or equivalent degree. Excellent mentoring and communication skills are necessary. Application materials should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and a 3-page research statement highlighting past discoveries and future vision. Reference letters will be requested during the recruitment process for selected candidates. Please upload application materials to: https://apply.interfolio.com/192850 Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190855": {
      "title": "Rosenwald Postdoctoral Fellowship in U.S. Foreign Policy and International Security (2027-28)",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Dickey Center & Dean of the Faculty Location: Hanover, NH The Davidson Institute for Global Security at the John Sloan Dickey Center for International Understanding at Dartmouth College invites applications for the 2027-28 E. John Rosenwald, Jr. '52 TU'53 Fellows Program in U.S. Foreign Policy and International Security . We welcome applications from any untenured scholar in history, political science, or related fields whose research bears directly on foreign policy, international security or international history. Fellows typically include recent Ph.D recipients as well as tenure-track faculty on leave from other institutions. We intend to appoint three historians and four political scientists for the 2027-28 academic year. Applications will be evaluated on the basis of (i) significance of the applicant’s research for advancing knowledge about U.S. foreign policy and international security; (ii) ability of the applicant to benefit from interaction with and mentoring from Dartmouth faculty in Government, History, or other relevant departments or programs; and (iii) ability of the applicant to contribute to Dartmouth’s community. Fellows receive competitive stipends, research support, and benefits, comparable to those offered by other fellowship programs. Fellows are expected to be in residence from September 2027 through June 2028 in order to participate in International Relations seminars and to contribute to Dartmouth’s community more broadly. Fellows who meet program expectations will have the option to extend the fellowship through the 2028-29 academic year. Please only apply to the program if you can commit to living and working in the Hanover, NH area throughout the academic year. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Applicants should hold a Ph.D, or equivalent degree, by the start of their appointment. Relevant disciplines include political science, history, or others if the applicant’s research relates directly to foreign policy, international security or international history. Please submit all materials electronically through Interfolio by Tuesday, December 1, 2026 at 11:59pm EST. 1. Cover letter, addressing all relevant aspects of the position description 2. Curriculum vitae 3. An abstract (200 words max.) describing the principal research project the fellow plans to conduct at Dartmouth 4. A statement (1,500 words max.) describing the applicant's broader research agenda 5. Writing sample (50 pp. max) 6. Two confidential letters of reference"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189760": {
      "title": "Neukom Postdoctoral Fellowship (2027-2029)",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Interdisciplinary Studies: Neukom Location: Hanover, NH The Neukom Institute for Computational Science at Dartmouth College invites applications for the Neukom Postdoctoral Fellowship. We expect to appoint several postdoctoral Fellows, with anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. Neukom Postdoctoral Fellowships are interdisciplinary positions for recent Ph.D.s, DMAs, or MFAs whose research interests or practice cuts across traditional disciplinary boundaries, and has some computational component, whether it be a framing concept for intellectual exploration or an explicit part of the work that is pursued. Qualified candidates should have a history of collaborative work across disciplines, while also showing evidence of independence and initiative. For information about current Neukom Fellows, please visit: https://neukom.dartmouth.edu/research/neukom-postdoctoral-fellows/current-fellows . The Neukom Postdoctoral Fellowships are two-year full-time appointments with the possibility of a third year extension. Other than standard research trips, Fellows are expected to be in residence at Dartmouth College. Neukom Fellows will be mentored by faculty in two departments at Dartmouth College, take up residence in one department, and will teach one seminar course each year on a subject of their interest. Applicants should contact prospective mentors before applying. Successful applications usually include agreement from current faculty to serve as mentors. The fellowship salary for 2027-2029 will be $70,000 per year (pro-rated in the event of early departure). Fellowships come with an annual research stipend of $5,000 per year. When warranted, additional funds maybe available to suppliment including the CompX Faculty grant . Fellows are eligible for certain benefits, as well as professional development opportunities with the Guarini School of Graduate and Advanced Studies , including their community initiatives . Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants are welcome to address in their materials how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. The Neukom Fellows Program and the Neukom Institute are made possible by a generous gift from Mr. William H. Neukom, Dartmouth College Class of 1964. Applicants must have a Ph.D., DMA, or MFA in any discipline, or have a degree conferred no later than September 1, 2027. Selection criteria include: research interests strongly intersecting with the theme of computation; demonstrated interest in multidisiplinary research; evidence of ability to think outside traditional paradigms; and potential to contribute to an interdisciplinary community of scholars. Please submit the following materials electronically via Interfolio: Curriculum vitae Publications list (uploaded separately from CV) Research Statement: describe your interests, including a short description of the research you would like to pursue as a Neukom Fellow (maximum 2 pages) Research Plan: describe the departments and Dartmouth faculty you would be interested in working with and why the opportunity to engage with multiple departments would enhance your work. Applicants are strongly encouraged to seek out connections with and contact potential Dartmouth mentors as they draft their applications. (maximum 1 page) Three referees whose letters of recommendation speak to the aims of the Fellowship. The Interfolio portal requires the reference email address: referees will receive an email and have access to upload a letter; the applicant will not have access to this letter. Please make sure referees are aware of the December 1 deadline and are expecting the email from Interfolio. Letters that arrive after December 8 may not be included in review of your application. (Optional) A copy of one article you have written in English (or some comparable kind of creative work), either published or unpublished (Optional) A teaching statement (maximum 1 page) (Optional) A cover letter We will begin reviewing applications on December 1, 2026 and continue until the positions are filled. General inquiries should be directed to the Neukom Institute Associate Director, Christine Ellen ( Christine.Ellen@Dartmouth.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192287": {
      "title": "Mae and John Hueston Distinguished Professorship in Native American and Indigenous Studies",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Interdisciplinary Studies: Native American and Indigenous Studies Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Native American and Indigenous Studies at Dartmouth College invites applications for the Mae and John Hueston Distinguished Professorship in Native American and Indigenous Studies. We seek qualified candidates at the rank of tenured Associate or Full Professor whose research and teaching address any context for the study of Indigenous Peoples. We are especially interested in applicants whose work will contribute to the department’s ongoing commitment to Global Indigenous Studies. We welcome applicants from any disciplinary or interdisciplinary background. In addition to having a record of excellence in scholarship, qualified candidates will also have a strong commitment to undergraduate teaching. Dartmouth has a long-standing commitment to interdisciplinary studies and our department has long-established relations with other units at Dartmouth whose interests and engagement with Indigenous Studies intersect with our own. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. PhD in Native American Studies, Ethnic Studies, or a closely related field in the humanities or social sciences; or an equivalent terminal degree. A record of distinction commensurate with a tenured appointment. Responsibilities include research and classroom instruction of undergraduate students. Please submit all materials electronically via Interfolio. Letters may be addressed to Professor Melanie B. Taylor: Cover letter CV Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this position, please contact Professor Melanie Benson Taylor, Melanie.B.Taylor@dartmouth.edu . This position is full-time, in residence, non-remote in Hanover, NH. For all openings in Arts and Sciences , please visit our Faculty Recruitment page."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/169836": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar-Frumkin",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Department of Biomedical Data Science Location: Lebanon, NH The research team of Dr. Madelyn Frumkin is seeking a motivated postdoctoral scholar to join our dynamic and interdisciplinary team. The successful candidate will work on a 5-year, NIH-funded project focused on leveraging multimodal digital health data to improve understanding, prediction, and treatment of postoperative pain. The work sits at the intersection of translational pain science, psychological science, and data science. The postdoctoral scholar will have opportunities to lead secondary analyses of existing intensive longitudinal data sets, assist with the collection and management of multimodal digital health data, mentor graduate and undergraduate trainees, and advance their independent program of research. The successful candidate may also contribute to other projects in the lab, including the design and evaluation of digital interventions for chronic pain and chronic illnesses (e.g., cancer-related distress). The lab is housed within the Center for Technology and Behavioral Health (CTBH), a P30 “Center of Excellence” funded by the National Institute on Drug Abuse. CTBH has numerous opportunities for training and mentorship in digital health, novel data analytic methods, manuscript preparation, grant writing, and more. Opportunities for clinical hours and supervision are also available. For more information on the Frumkin lab, please see our website . Required qualifications: 1.        Candidates must have a PhD in psychology, data science, computer science, engineering, or a related field. 2.        Candidates must be proficient in quantitative data analysis, as evidenced by a track record of publications and/or presentations. 3.        Candidates must have strong written and verbal communication skills. 4.        Candidates must be willing to work primarily in Lebanon, NH. Preferred qualifications: 1.        Experience analyzing ecological momentary assessment and/or passive physiological data using R and/or Python. 2.        Clinical or research experience in the fields of chronic pain, mental health, and/or substance use disorders. 3.        Interest or experience in digital app design and deployment. Please apply online using this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/169836"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/172455": {
      "title": "Associate Professor Tenure Track",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Department of Epidemiology Location: Hanover/Lebanon, NH The Department of Epidemiology at the Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth and the Molecular Epidemiology Center for Biomedical Research Excellence invites applications for a tenure track faculty position. We seek an Associate Professor with experience in the design and conduct of large-scale prospective molecular epidemiologic investigations. The department further has emerging strength in Implementation Sciences and is also interested in applicants with independent research programs in implementation research using molecular epidemiology. The Department offers a dynamic, interdisciplinary, and highly collaborative setting with a commitment and infrastructure to support rigorous and impactful research and career advancement. Dartmouth faculty benefit from close collaborative relationships with other departments across the medical school and university, including with the College of Arts & Sciences, Thayer School of Engineering, and Tuck School of Business. In addition, Dartmouth is home to an NCI Designated Comprehensive Cancer Center with four research programs, including Cancer Population Sciences and the Dartmouth Center for Implementation Science . As a part of success in this role there are expectations for teaching and mentoring. Individuals will have opportunities to participate in multiple graduate programs (e.g., the Program in Quantitative Biomedical Sciences , Implementation Science , Integrative Neuroscience, the Dartmouth Institute for Health Policy and Clinical Practice and the Program in Molecular and Cellular Biology ). Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds , life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Candidates must have a Ph.D. and/or M.D. degree and relevant teaching and extramural research experience to meet the qualif ications of a tenure track associate professor at Dartmouth . Applicants with a track record of extramural research support are preferred. Applicants should upload a cover letter, a curriculum vitae (without impact factors), a description of proposed research (3 pages or less), a teaching statement, and a statement on how their teaching, research, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to advance diversity, equity, and inclusion at Dartmouth through the Interfolio link provided. Three referees should provide letters of recommendation. Consideration of applications will begin on May 18, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Dr. Caitlin Howe with any questions: caitlin.g.howe@dartmouth.edu, (603)-646-5419"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188572": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Molecular and Systems Biology",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Department of Molecular & Systems Biology Location: Hanover, NH The Department of Molecular and Systems Biology at Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor. We seek applicants who will direct an independent research program to address complex problems in molecular, cellular, or developmental biology, with an emphasis on neuroscience, regeneration, or mechanisms of human disease using approaches in genetics, genomics, epigenetics or metabolomics. Applicants with a demonstrated record of technology development and/or application of innovative approaches are encouraged to apply. Our department offers a collegial environment where faculty members benefit from collaborative interactions across campus, with foundational and clinical departments at Geisel, the School of Arts & Sciences, the Thayer School of Engineering, and the Guarini School of Graduate and Advanced Studies. A generous start-up package and access to state-of-the-art research facilities will be provided. Depending on areas of expertise, successful candidates will mentor and teach Dartmouth graduate students in PhD programs [Molecular and Cellular Biology (MCB),  Quantitative Biomedical Sciences (QBS), and Integrative Neuroscience at Dartmouth (IND )]. The Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth is located on neighboring campuses in Hanover and Lebanon, New Hampshire, surrounded by a vibrant, academic, and professional community offering excellent schools, lively arts, and an outstanding quality of life. Boston, MA, Burlington, VT, New York, NY, and Montreal, QC are all within a few hours’ drive. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. People with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives make the Dartmouth community diverse, which enhances academic excellence. In their cover letter, applicants should summarize the impact and importance of their past work and address how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences will be of benefit to the Geisel/Dartmouth academic community and advance Dartmouth's commitment to diversity in service of academic excellence. Review of applications will begin on August 15, 2026. Initial candidate selection is expected to start October 1, 2026. Candidates must have a Ph.D. and/or M.D. degree and relevant postdoctoral research experience. Applicants should upload a cover letter, their curriculum vitae (without impact factors) and a research statement (not to exceed 2 pages) to: apply.interfolio.com/188572 , and request letters of recommendation from three referees."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/174605": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Immunotherapy and Cancer Immunology (ICI) or Signaling, Genomes and Networks (SGN) Research Programs",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Dartmouth Cancer Center Location: Dartmouth Cancer Center, Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth The Dartmouth Cancer Center (DCC) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor at the Geisel School of Medicine to join either our Immunotherapy and Cancer Immunology (ICI) or Signaling, Genomes and Networks (SGN) Research Programs. Exceptional candidates at senior ranks may also be considered. We seek an outstanding and collaborative individual to develop an innovative research program in cancer immunology or cancer biology. Research areas of interest include, but are not limited to, cellular therapy, tumor microenvironment and myeloid cells, antibody therapeutics, immune checkpoints, cancer vaccines, and T-cell immunology, the study of vertebrate models of cancer, the development of therapeutic strategies for or resistance mechanisms of cancer, and studies of cancer signaling pathways. The successful candidate will be appointed into one of several academic departments at the Geisel School of Medicine based on the focus of their research program, including Microbiology and Immunology, Biochemistry and Cell Biology, or Molecular and Systems Biology. DCC, one of 57 NCI-designated Comprehensive Cancer Centers, offers a dynamic and interactive environment, with a commitment to research excellence commensurate with Dartmouth’s status as an R1 Doctoral University. The successful candidate will have access to thriving multidisciplinary graduate programs through the Guarini School of Graduate and Advanced Studies (e.g., the Program in Molecular and Cellular Biology, which contains a concentration in Cancer Biology, and the Program in Quantitative Biomedical Sciences). Faculty members at the Geisel School of Medicine benefit from highly collaborative and collegial interactions across Dartmouth, inclusive of other departments at Geisel, the School of Arts & Sciences, and the Thayer School of Engineering. In addition, candidates may benefit from interactions with three current NIH Centers of Biomedical Research Excellence (COBREs), Dartmouth's Clinical and Translational Science Institute (CTSA), Dartmouth’s supercomputing networks, clinical faculty and other resources at the Dartmouth-Hitchcock Medical Center, and a new Dartmouth Innovation Accelerator for Cancer, which supports commercialization of Dartmouth discoveries. A generous start-up package as well as access to state-of-the-art research facilities will be provided. Successful candidates are expected to develop a robust research program and excel in graduate student teaching. The Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth is located in the picturesque Upper Connecticut River Valley on the New Hampshire/Vermont border, and is surrounded by a vibrant academic and professional community offering excellent schools, lively arts, and superb quality of life in a beautiful, small-town setting. We are only a few hours away from Boston MA, Burlington VT, and Montreal, QC. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Dartmouth College is an equal opportunity employer under federal law. We prohibit discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, veteran status, marital status, or any other legally protected status. Applications are welcome from all. Dartmouth is committed to accessibility for its community. If you are an applicant with a disability and would like to request a reasonable accommodation to aid in the job application and/or interview process, please email: ADA@Dartmouth.edu . In the subject line, please state application accommodations and include the job number or title.  Someone from the ADA/504 Office will be in touch within 2 business days. For additional employment opportunities at Dartmouth College, please visit the Dartmouth Interfolio Job Board, the Office of the Provost, and the Office of Human Resources. Offers of employment are contingent upon consent to a pre-employment background check with results acceptable under Dartmouth policy. Please visit the Dartmouth policy portal for faculty handbooks and details of other Dartmouth policies. Applicants should have a PhD, MD, or equivalent degree with relevant post-doctoral training and a track record of research productivity. Application materials should include: a cover letter (additional instructions below) curriculum vitae a teaching statement a 3-page statement of research accomplishments and goals that emphasizes the cancer relevance of an applicant’s research and three letters of recommendation The cover letter should include: a summary of the impact and importance of past work a statement on how efforts in the candidate’s research, teaching, and engagement could benefit the Dartmouth community, comment on how the Dartmouth Cancer Center would benefit the applicant’s academic program and aspirations, and how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all Application materials should be uploaded electronically to Interfolio.  Review of applications will begin December 15 and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190553": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Malaney Lab",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Dartmouth Cancer Center Location: Lebanon, NH The Malaney Lab ( www.malaneylab.org ) at the Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth is seeking a Postdoctoral Fellow to join our team studying RNA-binding proteins (RBPs) in health and disease. Our lab investigates RBPs regulate RNA splicing, modification, export, translation, and stability, and how mutations in these proteins contribute to pathogenic states. We integrate cell biology, molecular biology, and mouse modeling techniques to uncover the mechanistic basis of RBP dysregulation in cancer and neurodevelopmental diseases. We foster a collegial, inclusive lab culture built on curiosity, scientific rigor, and teamwork. Mentorship and career development are typically tailored to each trainee's goals. The candidate will have access to state-of-the-art instrumentation and core facilities. They will be supported in leading their own projects, pursuing new research directions, applying for fellowships, presenting at scientific meetings, and preparing for a range of career paths. Salary and Benefits Salary follows the NIH postdoctoral pay scale and will be commensurate with experience. Dartmouth offers a comprehensive benefits package, including health, dental, and vision insurance, retirement contributions, and access to institutional wellness and professional development resources. Key Responsibilities Under the direction of the Principal Investigator, the candidate is expected to lead experimental projects dissecting the role of RNA-binding proteins in health and disease. Keep meticulous laboratory records and communicate effectively and transparently about experiments. Train and supervise students in laboratory procedures. Stay up-to-date on relevant literature. Analyze research data and contribute to publications. Participate in weekly meetings, help plan future projects, and foster an inclusive, supportive environment. Preferred Qualifications Experience with mammalian cell culture, CRISPR/Cas9-based cell line generation, and molecular biology techniques Experience with mouse models of disease The ability to design rigorous, well-controlled experiments The ability to troubleshoot experiments and establish new techniques within the lab Strong verbal and written communication skills Excellent organizational, record-keeping, and time management skills, with strong attention to detail Ability to work well with colleagues across different backgrounds and experience levels Minimum Requirements A Ph.D. in cell biology, molecular biology, cancer biology, or a related field (ABD considered with expected defense date) To Apply Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter describing research experiences, fit with the lab, and long-term goals A CV Contact information for three references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190845": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Kettenbach Lab",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Dartmouth Cancer Center Location: Lebanon, NH Postdoctoral Fellow Kettenbach Lab – Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth, Lebanon, NH The Kettenbach Lab ( https://kettenbachlab.org ) at the Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth is seeking a Postdoctoral Fellow to join our team studying Phosphoprotein Phosphatase in health and disease. Our lab investigates the molecular mechanisms underlying phosphorylation-dependent signaling in normal cells and tissues, as well as in disease. Protein phosphorylation is an essential regulatory mechanism that controls nearly all aspects of cell division and is commonly dysregulated in human cancers. Protein kinases and phosphatases are the direct effectors of phosphorylation signaling. We are using state-of-the-art approaches in proteomics, biochemistry, and cell biology to functionally dissect signaling networks regulated by the interplay between protein kinases and phosphatases. We are developing methods for quantitative mass spectrometry-based proteomics and phosphoproteomics to gain novel insights into these complex signaling networks by determining dynamic changes in protein expression, protein-protein interactions, and post-translational modifications. We foster a collegial, inclusive lab culture built on curiosity, scientific rigor, and teamwork. Mentorship and career development are tailored to each trainee's goals. The candidate will be supported in leading their own projects, pursuing new research directions, applying for fellowships, presenting at scientific meetings, and preparing for a range of career paths. Key Responsibilities Under the direction of the Principal Investigator, the candidate is expected to lead experimental projects to dissect the role of phosphoprotein phosphatases in health and disease by using and developing quantitative mass spectrometry-based proteomics and phosphoproteomics approaches. Keep meticulous laboratory records and communicate effectively and transparently about experiments. Train and supervise students in laboratory procedures. Stay up-to-date on relevant literature. Contribute to collaborative projects. Participate in weekly meetings, help plan future projects, and foster an inclusive, supportive environment. Salary and Benefits Salary follows the NIH postdoctoral pay scale and will be commensurate with experience. Dartmouth offers a comprehensive benefits package, including health, dental, and vision insurance, retirement contributions, and access to institutional wellness and professional development resources. Minimum Requirements Ph.D. in cell biology, molecular biology, biochemistry, or a related field. Preferred Qualifications Experience with mammalian cell culture, CRISPR/Cas9-based cell line generation, and molecular biology techniques. Experience with mass spectrometry-based proteomics and phosphoproteomics. Experience with research in the field of Phosphoprotein Phosphatases. Ability to work well with colleagues across different backgrounds and experience levels. To Apply Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter describing research experiences, fit with the lab, and long-term goals. A one-page description of research ideas expanding published research by the Kettenbach lab or others in the field of phosphoprotein phosphatases and/or mass spectrometry-based proteomics and phosphoproteomics. A CV. Contact information for three references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/156622": {
      "title": "Research Associate (postdoctoral researcher)",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH  Position Summary This postdoctoral position is part of a project funded by the NASA Sea Level Change Team  (https://sealevel.nasa.gov/science-team/overview/) recently awarded to Dartmouth College. Join this growing, multidisciplinary team to help advance new understanding of polar regions and improve projections of sea level rise. Position Description Now in its fourth iteration, the NASA Sea Level Change Team (N-SLCT) has the goal of improving our understanding of regional relative sea-level change on a range of timescales and including a diverse set of physical processes that interact and combine in potentially complicated ways across a range of timescales. The N-SLCT relies heavily on NASA satellite observations in addition to reanalysis and modeling efforts to advance our understanding of sea level change in the past, present and future. As part of this team, our project uses novel understanding and observations of ice-ocean interaction at grounding line boundaries, combined with high-resolution ocean and ice sheet modeling to improve the projections of sea level change in key sectors of West and East Antarctica and provide a comprehensive view of sea level change contributions from Antarctica in decades to come. We are specifically looking for researchers with a background in ice sheet modeling, ocean modeling, or other polar processes. Computational experience running numerical simulations is required for this position and experience in the development or improvement of software is highly desirable. This position will provide the opportunity to work with a team of collaborators with expertise in ice sheet modeling, oceanography, and remote sensing, as well as to take part in the NASA Sea Level Change Team effort. Compensation for this position is competitive. Applicants should have a PhD or plan to finish their PhD in glaciology, physical oceanography, Earth Sciences or a related field. Key Accountabilities Designs and conducts experiments to investigate evolution of ice and polar oceans. Performs numerical simulations of ice sheet contribution to sea level. Analyzes and interprets acquired data. Implements changes in numerical software as needed. Stays current on relevant literature. Conducts day-to-day research activities independently. Shares objectives, results, interpretation, and conclusions with the research team. Prepares, submits and shepherds journal manuscripts Presents results at international conferences Demonstrates a commitment to diversity, inclusion, and cultural awareness through actions, interactions, and communications with others. Performs other duties as required. Requirements PhD in a relevant field (physical oceanography, glaciology, Earth Sciences, engineering or a related field) with a publication history Experience running numerical simulations of Earth Science processes Interest in working on computationally intensive projects Demonstrated strong written and interpersonal communication skills"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/173248": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar - energy and industrial transition planning",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH  The purpose of this position is to help build and apply industrial system planning models to explore regional and national pathways, with a focus on multi-objective optimization to evaluate cost, labor, climate, and air quality tradeoffs. The postdoctoral scholar position is in the Sustainable Transitions Lab – an interdisciplinary lab focused on just energy and industrial transition planning – at Dartmouth College, Thayer School of Engineering. Key Accountabilities ● Serves as a major contributing member of an interdisciplinary team of systems modelers, social scientists, and data scientists exploring steel industry transitions. Helps design and implement industrial systems modeling and infrastructure planning studies. (65%) ● Authors or co-authors research findings. (20%) ● Mentors undergraduate and graduate students. (5%) ● Presents findings at conferences and engages with stakeholders. (5%) ● Assists with writing grant proposals and obtaining funding. (5%) Performs other duties as required. Minimum Qualifications ● PhD in relevant fields including energy systems engineering, operations research, computer science, decision analysis, environmental economics, environmental planning, geography, energy resources, or environmental engineering. ● At least four years of relevant research experience. ● Candidates with additional non-academic work experience, with nontraditional academic backgrounds, and/or from members of groups that historically have been underrepresented in science and engineering are encouraged to apply. ● Demonstrated research ability and vison, excellent written and spoken communication skills, and comfort working both collaboratively and independently. ● Expertise in optimization methods, data science, and programming. Preferred Qualifications ● Expertise in geospatial analysis and familiarity with geographic information systems software. ● Familiarity with modeling environmental and socioeconomic impacts. ● Familiarity with macro-energy systems models. ● Position may be fully or partially remote for all or part of the term, at the discretion of both the research associate and supervisor. Please email Professor Erin Mayfield your CV at: erin.n.mayfield@dartmouth.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187552": {
      "title": "Distinguished and faculty development chairs in Materials Science",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH The Thayer School of Engineering at Dartmouth invites applications for chaired positions in Materials Science and Engineering.  Exceptional early-career candidates at the rank of assistant or associate professor will be considered for the Vincent Faculty Development Chair, while outstanding mid-career and senior candidates will be considered for a distinguished professorship. We seek candidates with a doctorate in materials science, engineering or a closely-related field who show strong potential to lead an externally-funded research program targeting transformational advances in Materials Science and Engineering. Examples of research areas include but are not limited to computational modeling, advanced manufacturing, energy materials, quantum materials, and applications of ML/AI to materials development or processing. Dartmouth is a member of the Ivy League and consistently ranks among the world’s leading academic institutions. Home to a celebrated liberal arts curriculum and pioneering professional schools, Dartmouth has shaped the education landscape and prepared leaders through its inspirational learning experience. The College has forged a singular identity, combining its deep commitment to outstanding undergraduate liberal arts and graduate education with distinguished research and scholarship in the Arts and Sciences and its three leading professional schools — Geisel School of Medicine, Thayer School of Engineering, and Tuck School of Business. Furthermore, Dartmouth's PhD Innovation (PhD-I) Program is the nation's first doctoral-level program aimed at providing PhD students the skills and support necessary to engage in enterprising research and translate their work into the commercial sphere. For more information see http://engineering.dartmouth.edu . The successful candidates will be gifted teachers with motivation and expertise that complements the Thayer School’s interdisciplinary approach to engineering education. They will contribute to a distinctive research and education program addressing innovative technological responses to societal challenges. The chairs provide resources to help launch a high‑impact research program and foster leadership across the materials community at Dartmouth, leveraging the recently-upgraded Electron Microscopy Center ( https://www.dartmouth.edu/emlab/ ), WeFab cleanroom facilities ( https://sites.dartmouth.edu/microengineering/equipment/ ), machine shop ( https://sites.dartmouth.edu/mshop/about/equipment/ ), and Discovery Cluster high-performance computing facilities (https://rc.dartmouth.edu/hpc/#discovery-cluster). Review of applications will begin October 1st, 2026 and will continue until the positions are filled. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, complete CV, statement of research and teaching interests and philosophy, and contact information for three references via Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/187552. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Inquiries about the position should be directed to Professor Ian Baker, Ian.Baker@Dartmouth.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188410": {
      "title": "Faculty Positions in Biological and Chemical Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH Dartmouth Engineering seeks to fill multiple open-rank, tenure-line faculty positions at the multidisciplinary nexus of Biological and Chemical Engineering. Exceptional early-career candidates at the rank of assistant or associate professor will be considered for the Ralph and Marjorie Crump Faculty Development Chair, while outstanding mid-career and senior candidates will be considered for a distinguished professorship . We seek candidates with a doctorate in engineering, chemical or biological sciences, or a closely-related field who show strong potential to lead an externally-funded research program targeting transformational advances in Biological and Chemical Engineering. Examples of research areas include but are not limited to microbial engineering and bioprocessing as well as broadly enabling technologies such as ‘omics methods and modeling. These positions will promote collaborative science within the broader Dartmouth community, including the Thayer School of Engineering, Dartmouth Health , Geisel School of Medicine , Dartmouth Cancer Center , Chemistry , and Computer Science . About Thayer Thayer School of Engineering is a dynamic community of scholars and teachers committed to addressing critical societal needs and solving the world's most pressing challenges through ground-breaking research, technology innovation, and cultivating future generations of engineers and leaders via educational excellence in the classroom and the laboratory. As Dartmouth's engineering school, Thayer is responsible for undergraduate and graduate engineering degrees. Our engineering sciences major is distinguished by the close integration of diverse disciplines and the liberal arts, emphasizing hands-on experiential learning, and human-centered engineering. As a professional school of engineering, Thayer also offers an ABET-accredited Bachelor of Engineering degree, master degrees in engineering and engineering management and a PhD degree program . Dartmouth is a member of the Ivy League and consistently ranks among the world's great academic institutions. Home to a celebrated liberal arts curriculum and pioneering professional schools, Dartmouth has shaped the education landscape and prepared leaders through its inspirational learning experience. Dartmouth has forged a singular identity, combining its deep commitment to outstanding education with distinguished research and scholarship in the arts and sciences and its three leading professional schools — Geisel School of Medicine , Thayer School of Engineering, and Tuck School of Business . Furthermore, Dartmouth's PhD Innovation (PhD-I) Program is the nation's first doctoral-level program aimed at providing PhD students the skills and support necessary to engage in enterprising research and translate their work into the commercial sphere. For more information see http://engineering.dartmouth. edu . Successful applicants will be gifted teachers well-prepared to teach courses in Biological and Chemical Engineering with motivation and expertise that complements Thayer's interdisciplinary approach to engineering education. They will contribute to a distinctive research and education program advancing innovative technological responses to societal challenges. Positions provide resources to help launch a high‑impact research program and foster leadership across the broader biological and chemical research community at Dartmouth. Review of applications will begin October 1st, 2026 and will continue until positions are filled. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, complete CV, statement(s) of research and teaching interests and philosophy, and contact information for three references via Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/188410. Inquiries about the position should be directed to the search chair at faculty.hiring@thayer.dartmouth.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189128": {
      "title": "Research Associate – Flow Cytometry Instrumentation and Cell Therapy Monitoring",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH  Position Type: Full-time Location: Dartmouth College, Precision Optical Diagnostics Laboratory, Lebanon NH Department: Thayer School of Engineering/Dartmouth Comprehensive Cancer Center Start Date: Immediate Compensation: Commensurate with experience Position Summary The Precision Optical Diagnostics Lab ( https://sites.dartmouth.edu/georgakoudi-lab/ ) is seeking a full-time Research Associate to join an interdisciplinary research project focused on the development of custom flow cytometry instrumentation and data analysis methods to monitor cell therapy products in vitro, ex vivo, and in vivo. This position is part of an exciting translational research effort at the intersection of engineering, immunology, hematology/oncology, data science, cell therapy, and immunotherapy . Our laboratory is an engineering research lab located in a building contiguous with the hospital and integrated with the Dartmouth Comprehensive Cancer Center, creating a highly collaborative environment with direct connections to clinical and translational research. The Research Associate will work closely with engineers, immunologists, hematology/oncology researchers, clinicians, and data scientists to develop, test, and apply advanced technologies for monitoring cell therapy products. This is an excellent opportunity for a motivated candidate interested in biomedical innovation, translational research, and the development of next-generation tools for cancer therapy monitoring. Key Responsibilities The Research Associate will contribute to several aspects of the project, including: Supporting the development, testing, and optimization of custom flow cytometry instrumentation. Assisting with experimental design and execution for monitoring cell therapy products in vitro, ex vivo, and in vivo. Performing sample preparation, data acquisition, quality control, and troubleshooting related to flow cytometry-based assays. Developing, implementing, or supporting data analysis workflows for high-dimensional cytometry data. Conducting literature reviews to inform experimental design, technology development, and data interpretation. Collaborating with interdisciplinary team members, including immunologists, hematology/oncology investigators, engineers, and data scientists. Maintaining accurate and organized experimental records, protocols, datasets, and analysis documentation. Participating in lab meetings, project discussions, manuscript preparation, presentations, and grant-related activities as appropriate. Contributing to a collaborative, rigorous, and innovation-focused research environment. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in biomedical engineering, bioengineering, electrical engineering, mechanical engineering, computer science, biology, immunology, biophysics, or a related field. Strong critical thinking and problem-solving skills. Ability to work both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Excellent organizational, communication, and documentation skills. Willingness to learn new experimental, computational, and analytical methods. Demonstrated ability to review scientific literature and apply findings to research questions. Desired Qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas is strongly desired but not required: Flow cytometry, fluorescence-based assays, microscopy, optics, or biomedical instrumentation. Instrument design, prototyping, calibration, or troubleshooting. Programming or data analysis using Python, MATLAB, R,  or similar tools. Analysis of cytometry, single-cell, imaging, or other high-dimensional biological datasets. Immunology, hematology/oncology, cell therapy, immunotherapy, or cancer biology. Wet lab experience, including cell culture, immune cell assays, or sample processing. Engineering design, systems integration, signal processing, or quantitative biology. Interest in innovation, entrepreneurship, technology translation, or biomedical device development is a plus. Ideal Candidate The ideal candidate is intellectually curious, detail-oriented, and excited by interdisciplinary research. They should be comfortable learning across fields, communicating with collaborators from different scientific backgrounds, and contributing to both hands-on experimental work and quantitative analysis. A strong candidate will bring initiative, creativity, scientific rigor, and enthusiasm for developing technologies that can support advances in cell therapy and immunotherapy. Research Environment Our lab is an engineering-focused research group embedded in a highly translational biomedical environment. The lab is located in a building contiguous with the hospital and is part of the Dartmouth Comprehensive Cancer Center, providing opportunities to interact with clinicians, translational scientists, and researchers working at the forefront of cancer therapy. The successful candidate will gain experience in technology development, biomedical instrumentation, data analysis, and collaborative translational research. Application Materials Interested candidates should submit: Resume or CV Brief cover letter describing research interests, relevant experience, and career goals Contact information for 2-3 references Applications should be submitted through Handshake or sent to Irene.Georgakoudi@dartmouth.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193974": {
      "title": "Tanner Chair in Next-Generation Sensing and Integrated Systems for Healthcare",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH Thayer School of Engineering at Dartmouth invites applications for the Tanner Chair , a tenure-line faculty position within Dartmouth’s Academic Cluster for Improving Healthcare Outcomes through Sensory Technology . We particularly encourage applications from outstanding candidates for the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, including postdoctoral scholars ready to launch an independent research program,  while exceptional candidates for full Professor will also be considered. Dartmouth’s Academic Cluster for Improving Healthcare Outcomes through Sensory Technology brings together faculty across engineering, computer science, neuroscience, and medicine to develop next‑generation technologies for human health, performance, and longevity. The Cluster creates opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration spanning sensing and devices, computation, clinical research, and translation. We seek candidates who show strong potential to lead an externally funded research program targeting transformational advances in next-generation sensing and integrated systems for healthcare . We are particularly interested in candidates whose research creates connections across sensing and devices, circuits, computation, and systems, including integrated and closed-loop approaches that bring these capabilities together, with the potential to translate technological advances toward meaningful improvements in human health and performance. Research areas include, but are not limited to, bioelectronics and novel sensing technologies; healthcare-related analog/mixed-signal, energy-efficient, and RF/wireless circuits and systems; wearable, implantable, and integrated sensing systems; computational and intelligent sensing; and neuromorphic and bio-inspired sensing and computing. Candidates whose work bridges or connects multiple areas are especially encouraged to apply. The position offers opportunities to collaborate with faculty in the Improving Healthcare Outcomes through Sensory Technology Cluster and across Thayer School of Engineering, the Department of Computer Science, Geisel School of Medicine, and Dartmouth Health. We particularly welcome candidates whose research can create new connections and amplify existing strengths across these communities. We seek candidates who are committed to excellence in teaching and mentorship and whose interests complement Thayer’s interdisciplinary approach to engineering education. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching, advise and mentor students, and help foster an intellectually engaging and collaborative educational environment. The Tanner Chair provides an opportunity to develop a high-impact research program and contribute intellectual leadership to Dartmouth’s growing efforts in sensing, circuits, computation, integrated systems, and healthcare technology, supported by collaborative research communities and advanced facilities across Dartmouth. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in electrical or computer engineering, bioengineering, materials science, computer science, or a closely related field. Applicants should demonstrate an outstanding record of research achievement or strong potential for such achievement, the potential to establish and sustain an externally funded research program, and a commitment to excellence in teaching, mentorship, and collaborative scholarship. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, complete CV, statement(s) of research and teaching interests and philosophy, and contact information for three references via Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/193974. For full consideration, applications should be received by January 1, 2027 . Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries about the position should be directed to Professor John X.J. Zhang, john.zhang@dartmouth.edu. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter  how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193985": {
      "title": "Instructional Faculty Position in Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH Dartmouth Engineering seeks to fill an instructional faculty position at the assistant or associate professor level. Instructional faculty positions are non-tenure-line, promotion and sabbatical eligible positions, with a renewable 3-year term, and the option of a rolling contract upon successful review after 6 years. Instructional faculty are expected to teach 4-5 courses each year, advise students, and take on service responsibilities. While conducting scholarship is encouraged, a research program is not required. About Thayer Thayer School of Engineering is a dynamic community of scholars and teachers committed to addressing critical societal needs and solving the world's most pressing challenges through ground-breaking research, technology innovation, and cultivating future generations of engineers and leaders via educational excellence in the classroom and the laboratory. As Dartmouth's engineering school, Thayer is responsible for undergraduate and graduate engineering degrees. Our engineering sciences major is distinguished by the close integration of diverse disciplines and the liberal arts, emphasizing hands-on experiential learning, and human-centered engineering. As a professional school of engineering, Thayer also offers an ABET-accredited Bachelor of Engineering degree, master degrees in engineering and engineering management and a PhD degree program . Dartmouth is a member of the Ivy League and consistently ranks among the world's great academic institutions. Home to a celebrated liberal arts curriculum and pioneering professional schools, Dartmouth has shaped the education landscape and prepared leaders through its inspirational learning experience. Dartmouth has forged a singular identity, combining its deep commitment to outstanding education with distinguished research and scholarship in the arts and sciences and its three leading professional schools — Geisel School of Medicine , Thayer School of Engineering, and Tuck School of Business . Furthermore, Dartmouth's PhD Innovation (PhD-I) Program is the nation's first doctoral-level program aimed at providing PhD students the skills and support necessary to engage in enterprising research and translate their work into the commercial sphere. For more information see http://engineering.dartmouth.edu. Applicants should have a doctorate in engineering or a related field and a demonstrated potential to teach a range of mechanical engineering courses. Example areas of interest include, but are not limited to, systems, solid mechanics, mechanical design, and sustainable design. Candidates will be excellent teachers who embrace Thayer's interdisciplinary, collaborative, project-based approach to engineering education. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, CV, statement of teaching interests and philosophy, and contact information for three references via Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/193985 . Applications will be reviewed as received, and the application portal will close on 1/1/ 2027 . Inquiries about the position should be directed to the search chair Professor Vicki May at faculty.hiring@thayer.dartmouth.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195755": {
      "title": "Research Associate B (postdoctoral researcher)Evans Family Postdoctoral Fellowship Program in Polar Science at Dartmouth College",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Thayer School of Engineering Location: Hanover, NH  The purpose of this position is to conduct innovative, independent, and collaborative research that advances understanding of the polar regions and their changing environments. The postdoctoral scholar will develop and pursue a research project in polar science in collaboration with one or more Dartmouth faculty members. The Evans Family Postdoctoral Fellowship Program in Polar Science supports interdisciplinary research across areas including oceanography, glaciology, atmospheric science, sea-ice science, climate science, mathematics, and engineering. The position is based at Dartmouth College in Hanover, New Hampshire, and provides opportunities to leverage Dartmouth's research expertise, facilities, datasets, computational resources, and collaborative networks. ● Develop and conduct an independent postdoctoral research project addressing important questions in polar science, in collaboration with the proposed Dartmouth faculty host(s). ● Collaborate with researchers across Dartmouth and relevant external institutions, contributing to interdisciplinary research activities and the broader polar science community. ● Communicate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, scientific presentations, and other appropriate scholarly activities. ● Contribute to a collaborative and inclusive research environment and participate in relevant academic and research activities at Dartmouth. ● PhD in a relevant field, such as Earth sciences, oceanography, atmospheric science, glaciology, climate science, environmental science, mathematics, engineering, or a related discipline, completed by the start of the appointment. ● A strong record of research accomplishment appropriate to the candidate's career stage, including evidence of the ability to develop and carry out scientific research. ● Demonstrated ability to communicate scientific results effectively through written publications and oral presentations. ● A clearly articulated research proposal that addresses an important question in polar science and is feasible within a two-year postdoctoral appointment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189225": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant / Associate Professor",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Dartmouth Center for Implementation Science Location: Hanover / Lebanon, NH The Dartmouth Center for Implementation Science (DCIS) at the Geisel School of Medicine, a Center for Biomedical Research Excellence, invites application for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor to expand our implementation research and capacity building initiatives across the Dartmouth ecosystem. Successful candidates will establish or develop an independent, extramurally funded, andimpactful research portfolio in implementation science. Additionally, the ideal candidate will serve as a core implementation science methodologist on interdisciplinary research teams, teach one course in the MS in Implementation Science program, mentor graduate students, fellows, and investigators on implementation research design, data collection, and analysis techniques. DCIS has emerging strength in pmplementation sciences training and research. The Center offers a dynamic, interdisciplinary, and highly collaborative setting with a commitment and infrastructure to support rigorous and impactful research and career advancement. Department affiliation will be co-determined at the time of hire. Dartmouth faculty benefit from close collaborative relationships with other departments across the medical school and university, including the College of Arts & Sciences, Thayer School of Engineering, and the Tuck School of Business. In addition, Dartmouth is home to a SYNERGY CTSA, multiple COBREs, NCI Designated Comprehensive Cancer Center with four research programs, including Cancer Population Sciences, Center for Rural Health, Center for Health Equity and Global Health, Center for Technology and Behavioral Health, and the Design Initiative at Dartmouth focusing on human centered design. As a part of success in this role there are expectations for teaching and mentoring. Individuals will have opportunities to participate in multiple graduate programs (e.g., MS in Implementation Science , Dartmouth MS and MPH programs , and PhD programs). Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth's commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Candidates must have a Ph.D. and/or M.D. degree and relevant postdoctoral research experience. Applicants with a track-record of extramural research support are preferred. This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Applicants should upload a cover letter, a curriculum vitae (without impact factors), a description of proposed research (3 pages or less), a teaching statement, and a statement on how their teaching, research, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to advance diversity, equity, and inclusion at Dartmouth through the Interfolio link provided . Three referees should provide letters of recommendation. Consideration of applications will begin August 1 and continue until the position is filled ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/161499": {
      "title": "Protein Biochemist - Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Department of Biochemistry & Cell Biology Location: bioMT, Vail Building, Hanover, NH Position Purpose The Dartmouth Institute for Biomolecular Targeting (bioMT) is seeking an experienced Protein Biochemist to join its Core Facilities (Molecular Tools Core, and Molecular Interactions and Imaging Core). Core staff members actively contribute to and facilitate multidisciplinary research projects across the Dartmouth community. The successful applicant will possess knowledge and extensive experience in construct design, production, and biophysical characterization of recombinant proteins. They will have experience in multiple expression systems and in developing and optimizing purification workflows involving multiple chromatographic methods; experience with the ÄKTA pure system is a plus. Previous experience with structural analysis (x-ray, or cryoEM) of proteins and/or in proteomics is highly desirable. Other activities include, but are not limited to, data collection and reporting, equipment maintenance, and training of bioMT investigators. Key Accountabilities Research/Experiments (65%) Works collaboratively with core facility staff, faculty, scientists, and graduate students from across the Dartmouth community to express and purify recombinant proteins for use in research projects. Independently designs optimal constructs for protein production in E. coli, mammalian (HEK/CHO), and insect (Sf9/Hi5) cells. Independently optimizes expression conditions and develops purification protocols for diverse protein targets. Develops proficiency in laboratory equipment for protein expression, purification, and characterization, including AKTA systems, centrifuges, and a variety of analytical instruments. Gains experience with diverse biochemical and biophysical techniques, including SPR, ITC, fluorescence polarization. Supports proteomics projects by helping with sample preparation, LC-MS/MS applications, and data analysis. Prepares and optimizes protein samples for structure determination by X-ray crystallography or cryoEM to support structural biology research projects. As needed, supports eukaryotic cell culture services, including culture maintenance and transfection. Troubleshoots and recommends solutions to problems associated with experimental design, procedures, and equipment. Data Collection/Preparation (15%) Collects, analyzes, and interprets data resulting from expression and purification experiments. Performs literature searches to develop information of relevance to current projects. Prepares appropriate and understandable representations of data such as sequence files, graphs, charts, etc. Participates in the preparation of annual reports. Presents data at laboratory meetings, departmental retreats, and scientific conferences. Contributes to the preparation of scientific manuscripts by drafting portions of the text, preparing graphs, tables, and charts, etc. as needed. Laboratory (10%) Adheres to laboratory safety requirements including general, biological, and chemical/hazardous waste safety disposal methods. Develops SOPs and other documentation for common procedures and methods. Develops and tracks quality-assurance and quality-control metrics. Maintains hardcopy and computerized records associated with laboratory activities. Training (10%) Provides training in laboratory methods, procedures, and techniques to multiple levels of scientific investigators (graduate students, post-doctoral fellows, staff scientists and faculty). Develops instructional materials as needed. Tutors in week-long workshops for undergraduates and graduate students (~3x/year). Communication Communicates and coordinates closely with co-directors and core staff, including staff of the Molecular Imaging and Interactions Core (MIIC) within bioMT. Maintains productive collaborations with bioMT investigators. Cultivates a diverse, inclusive, and respectful environment. Performs other duties as assigned. Required Education: PhD or equivalent experience in biochemistry, molecular biology, bioengineering, or related field is required. Experience: 3-4 years of relevant research experience or the equivalent working in a laboratory is highly desirable. Skills & Knowledge High competency in basic laboratory skills (pipetting, weighing, sterile technique, and recordkeeping), molecular cloning, and one or more of bacterial, insect, or mammalian cell culture. Experience with protein purification methods and equipment; Previous experience with structural biology is a plus. Ability to learn, develop, and apply new techniques. Ability to work independently and apply critical thinking and sound judgment. Good organizational and time management skills; Ability to multitask Ability to draft clear technical protocols. Willingness to work with hazardous materials including biological agents and/or hazardous chemicals. Knowledge of computers and software applications. Excellent communication skills. Willingness to work closely with diverse groups of students and research staff, including the ability to manage and resolve conflicts. **Applicants with less than three years of relevant post-PhD employment experience will be reviewed as a Research Associate instead of Research Scientist. Please submit a CV, Coverletter, and 3 references for review."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195169": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar-Song",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Department of Biomedical Data Science Location: Lebanon, New Hampshire The research team of Dr. Li Song is seeking a motivated postdoctoral scholar to join our dynamic team. This is a full-time, on-site postition based in Lebanon, NH. We are seeking an excellent candidate in one of the three areas: Large-scale biological databases and web portal. Low-level data structures for searching large-scale genome and protein databases. Computational immunology for TCR/BCR modeling and antigen specificity analysis. More information about the Song lab can be found at: mourisl.github.io CV Contact information for three references Statement of research interest. One page maximum."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196307": {
      "title": "Economics Research Specialist in Behavioral Economics and Finance",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: School of Arts & Sciences: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Hanover, NH Posted: Oct 27, 2026 The Department of Economics at Dartmouth College invites applications for an Economic Research Specialist in the area(s) of finance, applied contract theory, industrial organization, development, and/or behavioral economics. The position will be to work on projects headed by Professors Apoorv Gupta and Jonathan Zinman . This opportunity is ideal for candidates looking to develop their empirical and theoretical skills in preparation for pursuing a graduate program in economics or finance. The fellow’s responsibilities include: Coding and analyzing large datasets using Python, R, and/or Stata; Supporting field experiment design and implementation; Supporting data acquisition strategies and implementation from various sources; Performing structural estimation; Conducting literature and market landscape reviews; Writing up results for presentations and publications; possibly also supporting survey design and implementation. Economic Research Specialist (ERSs) will work with department faculty supporting and conducting scholarly research and teaching in anticipation of advancing their skills and experience to prepare for the next stage of their careers. ERSs are expected to contribute to all stages of research – including research design, literature searches, conducting experiments, collecting and analyzing data and writing reports and papers. ERSs may also have the opportunity to contribute in the classroom or in laboratory sections of courses, helping undergraduate students with labs, review sessions, paper editing and grading certain assignments. This position is full-time for the Department of Economics at Dartmouth College with a start date as early as July 1, 2027. Appointments are typically for a 1-year period with the option of a 1-year extension. We pay a competitive salary of $60,000 per year and benefits. ERSs who are working in residence in Hanover, NH are eligible for Graduate Special Student status and the ability to take certain Dartmouth courses with the tuition paid by Dartmouth as a working condition fringe benefit. The benefit covers a maximum of 1 course per academic year the ERSs is employed and in residence at Dartmouth. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their application letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all. Required: Bachelor’s degree or equivalent, completed by the start of the appointment and preferably in a match-based discipline Strong motivation to learn! Attention to detail Resourcefulness and strong problem-solving skills Strong quantitative, data, and project management skills Strong teamwork skills: ability to take initiative and/or direction, as needed Facility with Stata, Python, or R, or equivalent Fluency and excellent communication skills in English Knowledge of GIS software, and facility with structural models and estimation is preferable Preferred: A long-term interest in pursuing research related to economics and economic policy An interest in pursuing a PhD in Economics This position is eligible for J-1 visa sponsorship for eligible candidates. Employment is contingent on authorization to work in the United States. 1.  Application letter that addresses: Relevant research-related experience Teaching or mentoring experience Preparation to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all Educational and career goals and how the position would advance them 2.  CV/Resume Name Email and phone/text contact Current position or academic affiliation Date of (expected) undergraduate degree Academic concentration(s) Include programming languages and/or software and levels of proficiency 3.  Most recent academic transcript 4.  Contact information for three professional references Review of applications will begin November 12, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. For questions about the position, please contact Van Nguyen at Van.Anh.Nguyen@dartmouth.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/171438": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Biochemistry and Cell Biology, Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth",
      "description": "Department: Dartmouth College: Geisel School of Medicine: Department of Biochemistry & Cell Biology Location: Hanover, NH Posted: Aug 26, 2025 Deadline: Nov 20, 2026 The Department of Biochemistry & Cell Biology at Dartmouth’s Geisel School of Medicine invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level. Exceptional candidates at senior ranks will also be considered. We seek outstanding scientists working in broadly defined areas of biochemistry, cell biology, or structural biology. The department offers a dynamic and interactive environment, with a commitment to research excellence and a thriving multidisciplinary Molecular and Cellular Biology Graduate Program . Candidates may also benefit from synergies with the COBRE Institute for Biomolecular Targeting , the Dartmouth Cancer Center (an NCI-designated comprehensive cancer center) and many other thematic programs at Dartmouth . A generous start-up package as well as access to state-of-the-art research facilities will be provided. Successful candidates are expected to develop an independent and innovative research program and to participate in interdisciplinary research and training. Applicants should have a PhD, MD or equivalent degree with relevant postdoctoral training. Excellent teaching and communication skills are necessary. Application materials should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statements of research accomplishments and goals (up to 3 pages), a teaching statement, and three letters of recommendation. Please upload application materials and ask referees to upload their letter electronically to: https://apply.interfolio.com/171438 Review of applications will begin on October 15, and continue until the position is filled. The Geisel School of Medicine at Dartmouth is located in the neighboring towns of Hanover and Lebanon in the picturesque Upper Connecticut River Valley on the NH and VT border. Dartmouth is surrounded by a vibrant, academic and professional community offering excellent schools, lively arts, and an unmatched quality of life in a beautiful, rural setting. Amenities associated with urban areas in Boston, MA, Burlington, VT, New York City, NY, and Montreal, QC are all within a few hours’ drive. Dartmouth is committed to academic excellence and encourages the open exchange of ideas within a culture of mutual respect. Dartmouth welcomes people with different backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives and believes that diversity in all its forms enhances academic excellence. Applicants should address in their cover letter how their research, teaching, service, and/or life experiences prepare them to serve Dartmouth’s commitment to academic excellence in an environment that is welcoming to all."
    }
  },
